<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hawaii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hawaiian Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hawaii is an archipelago in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and, since 1959, the 50th state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>. Settled by Polynesian voyagers probably between about 1000 and 1200 CE, its islands were ruled by rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> united them as the Kingdom of Hawaii by 1810. The kingdom was overthrown in 1893, and the islands were annexed by the United States in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hawaii.jpg" alt="Hawaii" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late eighteenth century the <b>Hawaiian Islands</b> were divided among rival paramount chiefs, and Kahekili II of Maui had extended his rule over Oʻahu and Molokaʻi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alaska and <b>Hawaii</b> became the 49th and 50th states in 1959, and the country also holds territories including Puerto Rico and Guam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Japanese emigrants built shrines in <b>Hawaii</b> and Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Hawaiian, ʻŌlelo Hawaiʻi in the language itself, is a Polynesian language of the Austronesian family, native to the <b>Hawaiian Islands</b> and the historic language of the Hawaiian people.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From these islands, after a long interval, voyagers set out to settle East Polynesia from about the 11th century CE, reaching the Society Islands, the Marquesas, <b>Hawaii</b>, Rapa Nui and New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Elman Service called chiefdoms “redistribution societies with a permanent central agency of coordination”, but when Timothy Earle restudied <b>Hawaii</b> he found the communities largely self-sufficient: chiefs handed out prestige goods to their followers rather than food to those in need.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
